return array(
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A California LMFT learns at the beginning of a session that the client is temporarily attending from Nevada. What is the best next step?',
		'option_a'       => 'Continue routine treatment because the client permanently resides in California.',
		'option_b'       => 'Verify whether the clinician is authorized to provide services to a client located in Nevada before continuing routine treatment, while maintaining California professional duties and assessing any urgent safety needs.',
		'option_c'       => 'End the therapeutic relationship immediately without assessing risk or discussing continuity of care.',
		'option_d'       => 'Continue the session as long as the client gives verbal permission.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => "Why B is correct: The client's physical location during the session creates the interstate-practice question. Before continuing routine treatment, the California clinician should verify whether Nevada law authorizes the service. California professional duties and Board jurisdiction do not disappear simply because the client is elsewhere. The clinician should also assess whether any immediate safety need requires a limited crisis response or continuity action while the legal question is being resolved.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: A California residence or treatment history does not automatically authorize practice when the client is physically located in another state.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Immediate termination without assessing risk or planning continuity may create avoidable client-welfare and abandonment concerns. The appropriate response is a professional pause and verification process.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Client permission is necessary for treatment but cannot create professional practice authority in another jurisdiction.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Verify the client's present location first, then determine what legal authority, safety response, and continuity steps apply.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which set of actions is required when telehealth services are initiated under California Board of Behavioral Sciences standards?',
		'option_a'       => "Verify the client's full name and present-location address, assess appropriateness, and change the platform password.",
		'option_b'       => 'Obtain a photo identification card, identify an emergency contact, and secure a Business Associate Agreement for every vendor.',
		'option_c'       => "Obtain telehealth consent, explain potential risks and limitations, provide the clinician's license or registration type and number, and document reasonable efforts to identify relevant local resources.",
		'option_d'       => 'Verify insurance coverage, record the first session, and identify the nearest hospital.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => "Why C is correct: California BBS standards identify a specific initiation set: obtain telehealth informed consent consistent with Business and Professions Code section 2290.5, explain the potential risks and limitations of telehealth, disclose the clinician's license or registration type and number, and document reasonable efforts to identify relevant resources, including emergency services, in the client's geographic area.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Full-name and present-location verification and appropriateness assessment are each-session duties. Changing a platform password is a security practice, not part of the BBS initiation set.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Photo identification and an emergency contact may be part of a practice policy, but they are not the universal initiation requirements listed by BBS. A BAA is conditional, not required for every vendor.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Insurance verification, routine session recording, and identifying one hospital are not the required BBS initiation set. Recording may create additional privacy and consent concerns.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Keep initiation duties separate from each-session duties and from additional risk-management practices.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'At the beginning of every telehealth session, a California BBS licensee or registrant must verbally obtain and document:',
		'option_a'       => 'A new written telehealth consent form.',
		'option_b'       => "The client's full name and address of present location.",
		'option_c'       => "The clinician's license number and professional biography.",
		'option_d'       => 'A newly selected emergency contact.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => "Why B is correct: At the beginning of every telehealth session, the clinician must verbally obtain and document the client's full name and the address of the client's present location. The present-location address supports both legal analysis and emergency response and is more precise than asking whether the client is \"at home\" or \"still in California.\"\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Telehealth consent is obtained and documented at initiation; a new written form is not required at every session.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: The clinician's license or registration type and number are disclosed at initiation, not repeated as an each-session verification item.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Emergency-contact information should be kept current according to risk and policy, but a newly selected contact is not required at every session.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Use a consistent opening prompt that obtains the client's full name and exact present-location address.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which statement accurately describes California telehealth consent under Business and Professions Code section 2290.5?',
		'option_a'       => 'Consent must always be written and renewed annually.',
		'option_b'       => "The client's participation in the session automatically establishes sufficient consent.",
		'option_c'       => 'Consent may be obtained after the first telehealth session is completed.',
		'option_d'       => 'Before telehealth is delivered, the provider must inform the client about telehealth, obtain verbal or written consent, and document that consent.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => "Why D is correct: Business and Professions Code section 2290.5 requires the provider to inform the client about the use of telehealth, obtain verbal or written consent before telehealth is delivered, and document that consent in the record. The statute permits either verbal or written consent; it does not require one universal form.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: The statute does not require that telehealth consent always be written or automatically renewed every year.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Participation alone should not be treated as automatic consent. The provider must inform the client, obtain consent, and document it.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Consent must be obtained before telehealth is delivered, not after the first session has already occurred.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Telehealth consent may be verbal or written, but it must be informed, obtained in advance, and documented.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A long-term client reports that they moved to Arizona one month ago, and the clinician had not previously recognized the location change. What is the most appropriate response?',
		'option_a'       => 'Continue all routine sessions while checking Arizona requirements later.',
		'option_b'       => 'Terminate services immediately without assessing risk or planning continuity.',
		'option_c'       => "Verify the client's exact present location and immediate risk, pause routine treatment until lawful practice authority is confirmed, and document the continuity plan.",
		'option_d'       => 'Ask the client to keep using a California mailing address in the clinical record.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => "Why C is correct: The clinician should first establish the client's exact present location and assess immediate risk. Routine treatment should pause until the clinician confirms lawful authority to serve a client in Arizona. The clinician should document when the move was learned, what legal and clinical questions were identified, what consultation or verification occurred, and how continuity of care will be protected.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Continuing routine treatment while postponing the legal review repeats the underlying problem and may extend unauthorized practice.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Abrupt termination ignores risk assessment, transition planning, and continuity responsibilities. A lawful pause is not the same as abandonment.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Using a California mailing address would create an inaccurate record and would not change where the client is physically receiving services.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: When a location change is discovered late, gather facts, assess safety, pause routine care, verify authority, and document continuity steps.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => "What is the primary reason to verify the client's present location during telehealth?",
		'option_a'       => 'It supports both jurisdictional analysis and emergency response.',
		'option_b'       => 'It is used only for insurance billing.',
		'option_c'       => 'It confirms that the client is participating from home.',
		'option_d'       => 'It improves the quality of the video connection.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => "Why A is correct: Present location serves two central functions. It identifies the jurisdiction whose practice rules may apply, and it gives the clinician usable information if local emergency assistance is needed. These purposes remain important even when the client is clinically stable and usually attends from the same place.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Payers may have location-related rules, but billing is not the primary clinical and legal purpose of each-session location verification.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: The client does not have to be at home. The clinician needs the actual location and must assess whether the environment is appropriate.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Location verification does not improve bandwidth or video quality.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Location verification is a legal-and-safety procedure, not merely an administrative detail.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which progress-note entry best documents a session in which the clinician learns that the client is temporarily out of state?',
		'option_a'       => '"Client out of state."',
		'option_b'       => "Omit the location because the client's permanent address is already in the record.",
		'option_c'       => '"Session held normally; no changes."',
		'option_d'       => 'Document the present location, risk and urgency assessment, practice-authority question, actions or consultation, continuity response, and follow-up plan.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => "Why D is correct: A defensible note should show the clinician's reasoning and actions: the client's actual location, risk and urgency assessment, the interstate-practice question, consultation or verification steps, the immediate continuity response, and the follow-up plan. This record supports client welfare and demonstrates that the clinician did not simply ignore the change.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: \"Client out of state\" is too vague to establish location, risk, decisions, or next steps.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: The permanent address does not replace the present-location address and omitting the change removes information needed to reconstruct the decision.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Stating that nothing changed is inaccurate when the client's location and legal context changed.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Document the facts, the clinical and legal analysis, the action taken, and the continuity plan.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which item is a strong telehealth risk-management practice rather than the exact California BBS requirement that must occur at the beginning of every session?',
		'option_a'       => "Verbally obtain and document the client's full name and present-location address.",
		'option_b'       => 'Confirm a backup contact method and technology-failure plan when clinically or operationally indicated.',
