		'option_d'       => 'Comply with applicable privacy, confidentiality, and security requirements.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => "Why B is correct: A backup contact method and a technology-failure plan are strong telehealth safety and continuity practices, especially when risk is elevated or the connection is unreliable. They are not the exact item that BBS requires clinicians to verbally obtain and document at the beginning of every session.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Full name and present-location address are explicit each-session requirements and must be obtained at the beginning of the session.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Assessing whether the client and the session are appropriate for telehealth is an each-session regulatory duty.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Compliance with applicable privacy, confidentiality, and security requirements is also part of the telehealth standard, even though it is broader than a spoken opening question.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Teach learners which practices are mandated by regulation and which additional practices strengthen safety and risk management.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => "Which item may appropriately be included in a practice's intake identity-verification policy but is not a universal California BBS requirement at every telehealth session?",
		'option_a'       => "Obtaining the client's full name and address of present location.",
		'option_b'       => 'Assessing whether telehealth is appropriate for the client and session.',
		'option_c'       => 'Reviewing a date of birth or photo identification when required by payer, agency, or practice policy.',
		'option_d'       => "Documenting the client's present-location address at the beginning of the session.",
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => "Why C is correct: A practice may reasonably use date of birth, photo identification, payer verification, or another identity method during intake when required by policy, setting, payer, or circumstances. California BBS does not make photo ID or date-of-birth review a universal every-session requirement. The universal each-session focus is the client's full name and present-location address, along with appropriateness and privacy/security duties.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Full name and present-location address are required at the beginning of every telehealth session.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Appropriateness assessment is an each-session duty, not merely an optional intake procedure.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Documenting the present-location address is explicitly required at the beginning of every session.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Separate a practice's broader identity-verification policy from the specific BBS each-session requirements.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which sequence best reflects a structured opening for a routine telehealth session?',
		'option_a'       => "Confirm the client's full name and present location, assess privacy and appropriateness, and confirm the backup plan when indicated.",
		'option_b'       => 'Begin the clinical discussion immediately and verify location only if a crisis develops.',
		'option_c'       => "Ask only for the client's permanent home address.",
