		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => "Why A is correct: A structured opening collects the information needed before clinical work proceeds: identity and present location, privacy and telehealth appropriateness, and any backup or safety procedure indicated by the client's risk and technology conditions. Making these steps routine reduces reliance on memory and improves emergency readiness.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Waiting for a crisis defeats the purpose of location and backup planning and can delay response.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: A permanent home address does not establish where the client is physically located today.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Location verification applies to every telehealth session, not only to clients already classified as high risk.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: The first minute of telehealth should be a consistent safety and appropriateness workflow.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'When assessing whether telehealth is clinically appropriate, the clinician should consider:',
		'option_a'       => "Only the client's diagnosis.",
		'option_b'       => "Only the client's preference for remote care.",
		'option_c'       => "Only the speed of the client's internet connection.",
		'option_d'       => 'Clinical presentation and risk, privacy, technology reliability, engagement, supports, and whether the level of care is appropriate.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => "Why D is correct: Appropriateness is a multidimensional and ongoing clinical judgment. The clinician should consider the client's presentation and current risk, privacy, technology reliability, ability to engage, available supports, emergency readiness, and whether telehealth can provide an appropriate level of care. No single factor determines the answer in every case.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Diagnosis alone does not show current stability, privacy, engagement, support, or emergency-response capacity.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Client preference is important but cannot override safety, lawful practice, or level-of-care concerns.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Internet quality matters, but it is only one part of the appropriateness assessment.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Assess the whole telehealth context, not one diagnosis, preference, or technology feature.",
