	array(
		'question_text'  => "A telehealth session disconnects during a suicide risk assessment, and no backup communication plan exists. What is the primary concern?",
		'option_a'       => 'The clinician may be unable to assess safety or reconnect promptly.',
		'option_b'       => 'The clinician may be unable to bill for a complete session.',
		'option_c'       => 'The client may be dissatisfied with the platform.',
		'option_d'       => 'The session may end earlier than scheduled.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => "Why A is correct: During a suicide risk assessment, a disconnection can interrupt the clinician's ability to evaluate danger, maintain contact, and activate help. A prearranged backup method and escalation plan reduce uncertainty and allow the clinician to respond promptly when the client cannot be reached.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Billing is secondary to the immediate clinical concern of safety and continuity.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Client dissatisfaction may occur, but it is not the primary risk during an interrupted suicide assessment.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: A shortened session is less important than loss of assessment and response capacity.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: A technology-failure plan becomes a clinical safety plan when risk is being assessed.",
