	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which example is primarily an administrative safeguard for electronic client information?',
		'option_a'       => "Installing a privacy screen on a clinician's monitor.",
		'option_b'       => 'Using workforce training, access-management procedures, vendor review, and an incident-response policy.',
		'option_c'       => 'Using encryption during electronic transmission.',
		'option_d'       => 'Keeping the office door locked during sessions.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => "Why B is correct: Administrative safeguards are policies, procedures, and management actions used to protect electronic information and guide workforce conduct. Training, access-management procedures, vendor review, and incident-response policies fit this category because they organize how the practice manages security risk.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: A privacy screen is primarily a physical safeguard because it limits visual access to information on a device.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Encryption is a technical safeguard that protects information through technology.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: A locked office door is a physical safeguard that controls access to the workspace.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Administrative safeguards govern people and processes; physical safeguards protect spaces and devices; technical safeguards control electronic access and transmission.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Before adopting a new telehealth platform, what is the best approach?',
		'option_a'       => 'Use the platform that is most familiar to clients.',
		'option_b'       => 'Select the least expensive platform.',
		'option_c'       => 'Rely on marketing language that describes the platform as secure.',
		'option_d'       => "Review what information the platform handles, who can access it, recording and transcript settings, safeguards, configuration, vendor role, and the practice's risk analysis.",
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => "Why D is correct: A responsible platform decision begins with a documented review of the information the system creates, receives, transmits, or stores; who can access it; whether recordings, transcripts, chat, or tracking features are enabled; the available safeguards and configuration; the vendor's role; and the practice's risk analysis. Security depends on both product features and actual use.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Familiarity can improve usability but does not establish privacy, security, or legal suitability.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Price is not a compliance or security test. A low-cost or free platform may or may not be appropriate depending on its functions and safeguards.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Marketing terms such as \"secure\" are not a substitute for reviewing documentation, settings, data handling, and contractual responsibilities.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Evaluate what the technology actually does and how it will be configured—not its popularity, price, or advertising.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which example most clearly represents protected client information in the context of this course?',
		'option_a'       => 'A publicly available article about psychotherapy.',
		'option_b'       => 'A blank intake form with no client information.',
		'option_c'       => "A client's name linked to a diagnosis and treatment note in the practice's electronic record.",
		'option_d'       => 'The public address of a mental health office.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => "Why C is correct: A client's name linked to a diagnosis and treatment note is individually identifiable health information connected to care. In a HIPAA-covered practice or business-associate context, it is PHI, and when maintained electronically it is ePHI. Even outside federal HIPAA status, the information remains confidential client information subject to applicable professional and state protections.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: A public article is not information about an identifiable client receiving care.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: A blank form contains no identifiable client information.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: A publicly listed office address is not an identifiable client's health or treatment information.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Think about the combination of identity and care-related information, not isolated words or documents.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A client joins a telehealth session from a shared family laptop in an open room. What is the best response?',
		'option_a'       => 'Discuss privacy risks, determine who may be nearby or have device access, and use a private location, headphones, or another appropriate adjustment.',
		'option_b'       => 'Assume the platform alone protects confidentiality.',
		'option_c'       => 'Permanently terminate telehealth services.',
		'option_d'       => 'Record the session so there is a complete record of the privacy concern.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => "Why A is correct: The clinician should assess both environmental and device privacy. A shared laptop may expose saved passwords, notifications, browser history, portal access, or session information, while an open room may allow others to overhear. Collaborative adjustments can include a private location, headphones, another device, closing notifications, or rescheduling when privacy is inadequate.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: A secure platform cannot control who is in the client's room or who has access to the client's device.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Shared-device risk does not automatically require permanent termination; the clinician should first determine whether reasonable safeguards can be implemented.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Recording introduces additional privacy and storage risk and does not correct the open-room or shared-device problem.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Platform security is only one layer; telehealth privacy also depends on the client's environment and device practices.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A HIPAA-covered practice uses a vendor that stores protected health information on the practice\'s behalf. What is the best action?',
		'option_a'       => 'No vendor review is needed if the product is widely used.',
		'option_b'       => 'A Business Associate Agreement is required with every vendor, regardless of what the vendor does.',
		'option_c'       => 'A Business Associate Agreement makes a separate security review unnecessary.',
		'option_d'       => "Determine whether the vendor is acting as a business associate, obtain a Business Associate Agreement when required, and also review the vendor's safeguards and system configuration.",
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => "Why D is correct: A vendor that stores PHI for a HIPAA-covered practice may be acting as a business associate. The practice should determine the vendor's role, obtain the required written assurances through a BAA when the definition is met, and separately evaluate the vendor's security safeguards and system configuration. A contract and a security review serve related but different purposes.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Popularity does not establish a vendor's legal role or demonstrate appropriate safeguards.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: A BAA is not required with every vendor. It depends on whether the vendor performs functions or services involving PHI on behalf of the covered entity or business associate.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: A BAA allocates responsibilities and required assurances, but it does not replace risk analysis, appropriate configuration, access controls, or ongoing vendor oversight.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Determine whether a BAA is required, then continue the broader privacy and security review.",
