	array(
		'question_text'  => "A client sends detailed suicidal thoughts to a clinician's scheduling-only phone after hours. What is the most important prevention lesson?",
		'option_a'       => 'Clinicians should never allow any form of text messaging.',
		'option_b'       => 'Communication policies must define approved channels, response times, emergency procedures, and documentation expectations.',
		'option_c'       => 'Clinicians must monitor all electronic channels continuously.',
		'option_d'       => 'The message should be deleted because the channel was not approved for clinical content.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => "Why B is correct: The scenario reveals a mismatch between the clinician's intended use of the channel and the client's expectations. A clear communication policy should identify approved channels, typical response times, after-hours and emergency procedures, what content should not be sent through ordinary text, and how clinically significant messages will be documented and addressed.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: A total ban on texting is not required for every practice. Texting may be used for limited purposes when risks and expectations are clearly managed.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Clinicians are not required to monitor every electronic channel continuously, and implying continuous monitoring can create unsafe expectations.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Deleting a safety-related message because it arrived through the wrong channel could destroy clinically important information and does not address the client's risk or the policy failure.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Digital convenience requires explicit boundaries, response expectations, emergency instructions, and documentation procedures.",
