	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Which technology-failure plan best supports privacy, safety, and continuity of care?',
		'option_a'       => 'Tell the client to try logging in again later.',
		'option_b'       => 'Instruct every client to call 911 after any disconnection.',
		'option_c'       => 'Agree on reconnection steps, a backup communication method, a safety check, criteria for escalation, and documentation and follow-up procedures.',
		'option_d'       => 'Move the conversation to a personal social-media account if the platform fails.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => "Why C is correct: A strong disruption plan defines what both parties will do: attempt reconnection, use an agreed backup method, check safety, determine when escalation is necessary, and document the interruption, actions, outcome, and follow-up. The details should be proportionate to the client's risk and known technology conditions.\n\nWhy A is incorrect: Simply trying again later provides no safety check, alternate contact, or escalation process.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Calling 911 after every disconnection is disproportionate and ignores the client's circumstances, risk level, location, and local response options.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: A personal social-media account creates boundary and privacy concerns and is not an appropriate default clinical backup channel.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: A predetermined, professional backup plan protects privacy, safety, and continuity when technology fails.",
